## Why This Is Asked
Advanced latency management in multi-tenant clusters requires concrete CRD-driven control with observable outcomes and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes CRD and controller reconciliation loops
- Prometheus-based latency metrics (p95) and windowing
- Mitigations: HPA scaling, admission throttling, proxy-sidecar rate limits
- Status, events, auditability, and rollback semantics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: reconciliation skeleton
function reconcile(spec, status) {
  const latency = queryPrometheus(spec.namespace, spec.serviceSelector, 'p95');
  if (latency > spec.budgetMs) {
    scaleOut(spec.namespace, spec.serviceSelector);
    enableRateLimit(spec.sidecarSvc, spec.budgetMs);
  } else {
    removeRateLimit(spec.sidecarSvc);
  }
  return { observedLatency: latency, breached: latency > spec.budgetMs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test canary deployments for this policy?
- What safety checks would you implement to avoid flapping or service disruption?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes CRD design, per-namespace policy enforcement, drift detection, and the reconciliation loop. It tests translating high-level security baselines into concrete Kubernetes artifacts, and how to observe, audit, and test automated remediation in a multi-tenant environment.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes CRD design and controller reconciliation
- Translation of high-level baselines to NetworkPolicy and PodSecurity policy fragments
- Namespace-scoped overrides via labels
- Drift detection and automated remediation
- Observability: events, metrics, audit logs
- Testing: idempotency, end-to-end, rollback paths

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton reconcile function (pseudo)
async function reconcile(baseline) {
  const ns = baseline.metadata.namespace;
  const desiredPolicy = buildPoliciesFromBaseline(baseline.spec);
  patchNetworkPolicy(ns, desiredPolicy.network);
  patchPodSecurity(ns, desiredPolicy.podSecurity);
}
```

